window.docContents[90].resolve({"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Nonlinear-Heat-Singularity-Profiles":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity","header":"6.7.1.6. Nonlinear Heat Singularity Profiles","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Nonlinear-Heat-Singularity-Profiles"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/The-analytic-language-of-weak-solutions/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--The-analytic-language-of-weak-solutions--Spaces___-exponents___-and-boundary-data":{"contents":"The parameters that matter stay in the theorem statements: the open domain\n, the derivative order , the exponent , the ambient dimension,\nand the measure. The completed graph-space model keeps derivative coordinates as\n classes, which is what separates an almost-everywhere class from any\nparticular continuous or pointwise representative.\n\n membership of every output does not by itself\ngive a bounded operator, and almost-everywhere agreement identifies a selected\nrepresentative with an  class without turning the class into a\npointwise function. Trace values and singular quotients such as\n are defined on the completed space\nby density and continuity, never by evaluating an arbitrary representative.\n\nBoundary theorems use a finite collection of\nboundary charts with controlled derivatives.\nEach chart flattens a piece of the boundary into a coordinate hyperplane;\ncutoff functions localize the function to those pieces and assemble the local\nextensions. Bounds on the charts and their inverses let us transfer Sobolev\nestimates between the original domain and the flattened coordinates. We ask\nfor these maps and bounds explicitly because the extension, trace, and\ncompactness proofs use them quantitatively. A separate geometric theorem\nconstructs this data for a bounded open set with  graph boundary. The\ninterval and the Euclidean unit ball are the concrete instances.\n\nZero boundary values come from closures of compactly supported smooth\nfunctions:  and the higher-order  model. The embedding\ntheorems carry their own exponent inequalities, which is what decides whether\nyou are in the subcritical, critical, or Morrey regime.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009The analytic language of weak solutions","header":"4.1.3. Spaces, exponents, and boundary data","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/The-analytic-language-of-weak-solutions/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--The-analytic-language-of-weak-solutions--Spaces___-exponents___-and-boundary-data"}});